---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Weather API Integration Demo | Mike Kelley"
  description="A working weather application demonstrating REST API integration, input validation, asynchronous JavaScript, server-side credential management, accessible status feedback, and technical documentation."
>
  <section class="page-hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">SOFTWARE DEMONSTRATION / API INTEGRATION</p>
    <h1>Weather API integration, from request to documented implementation</h1>
    <p class="lede">This project demonstrates how I approach a small software feature from both implementation and communication perspectives. The application accepts a US ZIP code, retrieves weather data through external APIs, and presents the response in a clear interface. Supporting documentation explains the request flow, architecture, security considerations, and design decisions.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="#try-it">Explore the application</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href={`${base}demos/openweather/`}>Read the walkthrough</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/MikeKelley89/openWeatherApi" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Review source code ↗</a>
    </div>
    <div class="proof-row">
      <div><strong>JavaScript + jQuery</strong><span>Form handling, asynchronous requests, and interface updates</span></div>
      <div><strong>OpenWeather APIs</strong><span>ZIP-code geocoding and weather data retrieval</span></div>
      <div><strong>Technical documentation</strong><span>Architecture, implementation details, and trade-offs</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="walkthrough-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">FEATURED DOCUMENTATION SAMPLE</p>
      <h2 id="walkthrough-title">OpenWeather API: Technical Implementation Walkthrough</h2>
      <p>The live application shows the integration working. The companion walkthrough shows how I explain the implementation: the API request sequence, ZIP-code validation, asynchronous JavaScript, server-side credential handling, architecture, and design trade-offs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">DOCUMENTATION / IMPLEMENTATION</span>
        <h3>Read the technical walkthrough</h3>
        <p>Follow the implementation and the decisions behind it, including request flow, security considerations, and limitations.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}demos/openweather/`}>Open the complete walkthrough ↗</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">INTERACTIVE / SOURCE</span>
        <h3>Explore the working application</h3>
        <p>Try the ZIP-code weather interface below, or inspect the source to see how the documented approach is implemented.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="#try-it">Try the live application ↓</a></p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/MikeKelley89/openWeatherApi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View source on GitHub ↗</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="try-it">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">INTERACTIVE APPLICATION</p>
        <h2>Retrieve local weather by US ZIP code</h2>
        <p>Enter a five-digit US ZIP code to resolve the location, retrieve current conditions and a daily summary, and display a weather alert when one is returned by the API.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="weather-form">
        <form id="localWeather">
          <label for="zip">US ZIP code</label>
          <div class="input-row">
            <input id="zip" name="zip" type="text" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="postal-code" pattern="[0-9]{5}" maxlength="5" placeholder="e.g. 84010" aria-describedby="zipHelp zipStatus" required />
            <button id="findWeather" class="button primary" type="submit" disabled>Retrieve weather</button>
          </div>
          <p class="form-help" id="zipHelp">Enter five digits. ZIP+4 is not supported.</p>
          <p class="form-help error-text" id="zipStatus" hidden>Enter a valid five-digit US ZIP code.</p>
        </form>
        <p class="weather-status" id="weatherStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite">Enter a ZIP code to retrieve local weather data.</p>

        <div class="weather-result" id="weatherError" role="alert" hidden>
          <strong>Unable to retrieve weather</strong>
          <p><span id="errorCode"></span><span id="errorMsg"></span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="weather-result" id="weatherAlert" role="alert" hidden>
          <p class="eyebrow">WEATHER ALERT</p>
          <h3 id="event">Weather alert</h3>
          <p><strong>Issued by:</strong> <span id="sender_name"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Starts:</strong> <span id="start"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Ends:</strong> <span id="end"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Description:</strong> <span id="description"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Type:</strong> <span id="tags"></span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="weather-result" id="weatherResults" hidden>
          <h3 id="conditions">Current conditions</h3>
          <div class="weather-main">
            <div>
              <div class="weather-temp"><span id="main_temp">--</span><span aria-label="degrees Fahrenheit">°F</span></div>
              <p>Current temperature</p>
            </div>
            <div class="weather-meta">
              <span>Wind speed<strong><span id="wind_speed">--</span> mph</strong></span>
              <span>Wind direction<strong><span id="wind_direction">--</span>°</strong></span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p id="weather_conditions"></p>
        </div>
        <p class="small-note">This portfolio project is intended to demonstrate API integration and technical communication, not to provide emergency weather information. The browser calls a Cloudflare Worker, which stores the OpenWeather credential as a server-side secret rather than exposing it in client-side code. Public use of an endpoint like this also requires appropriate usage limits and monitoring.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">DATA FLOW</p>
      <h2>From ZIP code to weather response</h2>
      <p>The weather API requires geographic coordinates, so the application first resolves the submitted ZIP code and then uses the resulting latitude and longitude to retrieve weather data.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pipeline">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Validate input</h3><p>Confirm that the submitted value contains exactly five digits before sending a request.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Resolve location</h3><p>Use OpenWeather's Geocoding API to obtain the location name and geographic coordinates.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Retrieve weather</h3><p>Request data from One Call API 3.0 using the coordinates, imperial units, and only the data categories needed by the interface.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Present results</h3><p>Render the current temperature, wind information, daily summary, and first available weather alert.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">REQUEST ARCHITECTURE</p>
      <h2>Server-side credential handling</h2>
      <p>The browser sends the ZIP code to a narrowly scoped Cloudflare Worker. The Worker validates the request, applies rate limiting, calls OpenWeather using a server-side secret, and returns only the location and weather fields required by the interface.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="architecture-flow" role="img" aria-label="Browser sends ZIP code to Cloudflare Worker. Worker validates and rate limits the request, calls OpenWeather Geocoding and One Call APIs using a server-side secret, then returns weather data for display.">
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>01</span><strong>Browser</strong><p>Validate the ZIP code and request <code>/weather?zip=84010</code>.</p></article>
      <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>02</span><strong>Cloudflare Worker</strong><p>Validate origin, HTTP method, input, and request rate.</p></article>
      <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>03</span><strong>OpenWeather</strong><p>Resolve coordinates, retrieve weather data, and return a reduced response.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="small-note">The <code>OPENWEATHER_API_KEY</code> secret is available only to the Worker runtime. CORS controls browser access; it is not authentication. Rate limiting and provider-side usage limits are complementary controls.</p>
    <p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/MikeKelley89/openWeatherApi/tree/main/worker" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Review the Worker implementation and setup documentation ↗</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS</p>
        <h2>Engineering decisions reflected in the interface</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-number">01 / VALIDATION</span><h3>Validate before requesting data</h3><p>The interface checks ZIP-code format and keeps the submit control disabled until the input is valid. The server independently validates incoming requests, since client-side checks alone are not a security boundary.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-number">02 / REQUEST HANDLING</span><h3>Manage asynchronous states</h3><p>The interface communicates request progress, restores the submit control after completion, and presents errors separately from successful results.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-number">03 / ACCESSIBILITY</span><h3>Make status changes perceivable</h3><p>The form uses a visible label, status updates use a live region, and errors and results appear in the page with text that does not depend on color alone.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">TECHNICAL COMMUNICATION</p>
        <h2>Documentation that explains both what and why</h2>
        <p>The accompanying walkthrough describes the request sequence, key implementation details, architectural choices, and known limitations. It is designed to help readers understand the system without needing to infer how the components fit together.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}demos/openweather/`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the integrated code walkthrough ↗</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="code-panel">
        <pre class="language-javascript"><code class="language-javascript">// 1. Resolve a ZIP code to coordinates
const location = await geocode(zip);

// 2. Request weather for those coordinates
const weather = await getWeather(location.lat, location.lon);

// 3. Present the response
renderWeather(weather.current, weather.daily, weather.alerts);</code></pre>
        <p class="small-note">Simplified request flow for illustration.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">SCOPE AND NEXT STEPS</p>
        <h2>Designed as a focused portfolio project</h2>
        <p>The current implementation demonstrates the core request flow and selected safeguards. The following enhancements would be appropriate before using the service as a production weather application.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="check-grid">
        <p><strong>Operational monitoring</strong><br />Track request volume, upstream failures, and usage against provider limits; strengthen abuse controls as needed.</p>
        <p><strong>Location validation</strong><br />Handle ZIP codes that do not resolve, in addition to validating their format.</p>
        <p><strong>Comprehensive alert presentation</strong><br />Display and distinguish all returned alerts rather than only the first one.</p>
        <p><strong>Resilience and test coverage</strong><br />Test empty responses, rate limits, timeouts, and partial API data, and define consistent behavior for each case.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="small-note" style="margin-top:26px">These items define a practical path for further development and are not represented as completed features.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="contact-strip">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">MORE FROM MY PORTFOLIO</p>
        <h2>Technical writing backed by hands-on implementation</h2>
        <p>My work spans software and API documentation, docs-as-code workflows, content systems, and practical front-end examples. I focus on making technical systems understandable to the people who use, maintain, and support them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}writing/`}>View writing samples</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={base}>Back to portfolio</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="/scripts/weather-demo.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
